\contentsline {table}{\numberline {7.1}{\ignorespaces NL-to-SQL vanilla baselines on the four rebuilt test sets, both checkpoints and both prompts. Execution accuracy (fraction of cases). The paper's vanilla figures (Table~3, Llama-3.1-8B) are set beside our zero-shot rows only, because the paper does not state its test prompt.}}{36}{table.7.1}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {7.2}{\ignorespaces Core study on Llama-3.1-8B base: every seed and round of Table-Specialist (TS) and no-validation (NV).}}{37}{table.7.2}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {7.3}{\ignorespaces NL-to-SQL summary statistics over seeds for every approach and round. SD is the sample standard deviation ($n-1$); it is omitted when only one seed exists. Vanilla is a single deterministic run.}}{38}{table.7.3}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {7.4}{\ignorespaces Algorithm~3 outcomes for every validated round (3,000 tables per round). Each question yields one SQL--Scala pair; a pair is kept only if both programs run and agree on all 10 row samples. Rejection reasons are mutually exclusive and are assigned in the order the validator checks them.}}{41}{table.7.4}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {7.5}{\ignorespaces Core study on Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct: TS on seeds 1--3 and NV on seed~1, every round.}}{43}{table.7.5}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {8.1}{\ignorespaces Extension~2 labelling (base, round~1): 4 candidates per question (TS's greedy pair + 3 vanilla samples at temperature 0.7); the first candidate that passes Algorithm~3 is chosen.}}{47}{table.8.1}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {8.2}{\ignorespaces Extension~2 test scores per seed (base, round~1), beside TS and NV round~1 on the same questions.}}{48}{table.8.2}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {8.3}{\ignorespaces DPO tuning on dev sets only (zero-shot; WikiSQL 300, Spider single-table 411 within the prompt limit, WikiTQ 300). Selection rule fixed beforehand: test a variant only if its dev mean exceeds bestof4's.}}{49}{table.8.3}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {8.4}{\ignorespaces Repair pilot on seed~1's 300-table round~1 (291 questions, 41 pairs kept first pass): the same rejected pairs and diagnoses, repaired by base Llama-3.1-8B or by Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct.}}{50}{table.8.4}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {8.5}{\ignorespaces Repair-TS (Extension~3): rejected pairs repaired once by Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct and validated again with Algorithm~3 unchanged. Per-reason columns give recovered / attempted; the last three give which side's new code passed.}}{51}{table.8.5}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {8.6}{\ignorespaces Repair-TS on base, every seed and round (repairs written by Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct).}}{52}{table.8.6}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {8.7}{\ignorespaces Repair-TS on Instruct, every seed and round (the Instruct model is generator and repairer).}}{52}{table.8.7}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {8.8}{\ignorespaces Same-data control: TS round~1's kept pairs plus as many extra validated first-pass pairs (from another seed's TS round~1, on tables this seed never used) as repair-TS round~1 recovered.}}{53}{table.8.8}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {8.9}{\ignorespaces Self-consistency temperature tuning on dev sets (base TS round~3 seed~1 adapter, zero-shot, greedy + 8 samples). The highest dev mean was taken to the test sets.}}{55}{table.8.9}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {8.10}{\ignorespaces Execution-based self-consistency on the test sets (greedy + 8 samples at T = 1.0, majority vote over execution results; failed or empty results dropped; gold never used). ``Greedy'' is the greedy answer of the same run.}}{55}{table.8.10}%
